WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:04.590
Even though we think we understand what we're
seeing and hearing in many of the scenes in

00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:08.730
the trailer for The Rise of Skywalker, there's
a pretty good chance the final film might

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:11.880
present something very different from the
conclusions we've drawn.

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:15.829
The footage may be edited a certain way, or
digitally altered, or just presented in a

00:00:15.829 --> 00:00:17.980
context that hides the bigger picture.

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:21.760
With that in mind, it's time to talk about
all of the possibilities contained in this

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:24.460
footage — and some of the ways in which
we could be deceived by it.

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:30.420
The trailer for The Rise of Skywalker begins
with an unforgettable sequence in which Rey

00:00:30.429 --> 00:00:35.290
stands alone on a desert planet and waits
as a First Order TIE fighter approaches.

00:00:35.290 --> 00:00:39.580
When it enters her path, she ignites her lightsaber,
then runs until she's between its wings, and

00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:40.160
leaps.

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:44.460
The implication here is that she's preparing
to cut the ship in two with her blade aimed

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:49.120
right for the cockpit, and the even deeper
implication is that Kylo Ren is the pilot

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:49.800
of the ship.

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:54.280
That may indeed be the case, but look closely
at that ship and it becomes clear that it's

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:56.580
not one we've necessarily seen before.

00:00:57.100 --> 00:01:01.960
In The Last Jedi, Kylo flew a TIE Silencer
with an all-black paint job, red-tinted windows

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:03.540
and a very angular cockpit.

00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:08.760
This ship looks more like a traditional TIE
Interceptor with a brighter paint job around

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:11.160
the rounded, more typical-looking TIE cockpit
design.

00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:15.980
We also never get a look at who's at the controls
— we get a shot of some hands, but nothing

00:01:15.980 --> 00:01:18.580
that definitively reveals the pilot's identity.

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:21.520
Is this an epic rematch between Rey and Ren?

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:22.920
Or just a marketing fakeout?

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:29.020
In Star Wars: The Last Jedi, Kylo Ren smashes
his helmet to pieces in a fit of rage after

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:32.840
Supreme Leader Snoke, like a true disciple
of the pick-up artist, negs him for wearing

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:33.340
it.

00:01:33.820 --> 00:01:37.480
"Take that ridiculous thing off."

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:43.270
The loss of the helmet is about Kylo's anger,
yes, but also seems to be part of his growing

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:45.359
philosophy of "letting the past die."

00:01:45.359 --> 00:01:50.190
Despite this, the Rise of Skywalker teaser
trailer shows that same helmet being pieced

00:01:50.190 --> 00:01:53.539
back together by a pair of mysteriously furry
hands.

00:01:53.539 --> 00:01:56.829
When we see Kylo Ren in the teaser, though,
he's not wearing the helmet.

00:01:56.829 --> 00:02:01.179
He's charging into battle with his head uncovered,
burying opponents with his lightsaber.

00:02:01.179 --> 00:02:05.409
Now, it's obviously possible that the helmet
simply isn't reforged yet when that scene

00:02:05.409 --> 00:02:09.780
takes place, but it's also possible that Kylo
isn't even the one who decides the helmet

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:10.920
needs to be put back together.

00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:13.840
Unless his hands got super hairy under those
gloves.

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:19.640
There have been two Death Stars in the Star
Wars galaxy so far, both destroyed by the

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:21.500
Rebellion at different locations.

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:25.970
The first exploded near Yavin IV thanks to
Luke Skywalker's careful aim, and the second

00:02:25.970 --> 00:02:30.800
was destroyed over the forest moon of Endor
by Lando Calrissian, Wedge Antilles, and the

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:31.810
Rebel fleet.

00:02:31.810 --> 00:02:39.190
In The Rise of Skywalker teaser, we see Rey,
Finn, Poe, Chewbacca, C-3PO, BB-8, and D-0

00:02:39.190 --> 00:02:42.670
looking out over what seems to be a fragment
of a destroyed Death Star.

00:02:42.670 --> 00:02:46.910
The image is immediately followed by the sound
of Emperor Palpatine laughing, which would

00:02:46.910 --> 00:02:50.780
suggest the Death Star wreckage is from the
second station — the one Palpatine died

00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:51.280
on.

00:02:51.880 --> 00:02:53.280
That's not necessarily the case, though.

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:58.260
Yavin IV is a planet full of jungles, while
the forest moon of Endor is full of… well,

00:02:58.260 --> 00:02:58.760
forests.

00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:02.420
The footage we see features a vast sea and
sparse grassy cliffs.

00:03:03.020 --> 00:03:05.540
Maybe this wreckage landed on another planet
entirely.

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:10.400
This could even be a third or fourth Death
Star we never saw in the movies at all, the

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:13.580
remnants of some kind of abandoned super-weapon
experiment.

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:18.880
Whatever the case, we can't necessarily assume
we know which Death Star that is...and that's

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:19.400
exciting.

00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:25.280
Fans around the world are excited about the
potential return of Emperor Palpatine, signified

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:27.880
by his fantastic laugh at the end of the trailer.

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:32.690
Admit it, no matter how you feel about Star
Wars these days, everyone is excited for more

00:03:32.690 --> 00:03:33.180
of this guy:

00:03:34.600 --> 00:03:36.380
"No… no…

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:40.860
NO NO YOU WILL DIE!"

00:03:41.660 --> 00:03:44.680
That said, we just don't know exactly how
he might actually return.

00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:49.370
A Palpatine clone would allow him to manifest
in a physical form, though it wouldn't really

00:03:49.370 --> 00:03:54.300
be "him" anymore in the strictest sense, while
a Force ghost would allow him to retain all

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:58.140
of his memories and past deeds with less ability
to interact with the physical world.

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:03.080
All of that said, it's possible the Emperor
isn't actually returning at all.

00:04:03.080 --> 00:04:07.910
For all the hype surrounding his resurgence,
it's possible Rey or Kylo or both could simply

00:04:07.910 --> 00:04:12.500
see him in a vision, or that our heroes might
find an old hologram recording of him somewhere

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:13.700
in those Death Star ruins.

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:17.960
Now let's talk about that title, The Rise
of Skywalker.

00:04:18.760 --> 00:04:23.240
If we begin with the argument that the simplest
explanation is often the best, then that means

00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:26.720
we gotta discuss Ben Solo, a.k.a. Kylo Ren.

00:04:27.050 --> 00:04:31.620
Much of The Last Jedi was devoted to examining
his mission to finish his grandfather's work

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:35.740
of wiping out the Jedi, and to Rey's belief
that she could redeem Kylo Ren.

00:04:36.250 --> 00:04:40.400
If Ben really is redeemed in this film, then
it's possible that he might once more take

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:45.380
the true name of his grandfather and his uncle
who tried to save him, and become Ben Skywalker.

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:49.560
That's the simplest route to an explanation
for the title… but it also feels pretty

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:50.100
unlikely.

00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:52.200
A movie made by J.J.

00:04:52.210 --> 00:04:56.629
"Mystery Box" Abrams in which the key villain
converts to the side of good again isn't going

00:04:56.629 --> 00:05:00.539
to telegraph that twist right there in the
title, so we should maintain our skepticism

00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:01.680
with regard to this theory.

00:05:03.090 --> 00:05:07.260
If we set aside the "Kylo Ren is redeemed"
version of the title's interpretation, that

00:05:07.271 --> 00:05:11.970
naturally leads us to turn to Rey, the desert
scavenger turned Force user whose origins

00:05:11.970 --> 00:05:13.800
remain shrouded in mystery.

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:18.130
The Force Awakens teased that we would eventually
learn Rey's origin, even throwing in a big

00:05:18.130 --> 00:05:21.600
Force vision the first time she touched Luke
Skywalker's old lightsaber.

00:05:22.150 --> 00:05:27.220
This prompted waves of speculation that Rey
was a secret Skywalker, or perhaps a secret

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:28.340
Solo or Kenobi.

00:05:28.540 --> 00:05:33.190
The Last Jedi seemed to shove those theories
aside when Kylo Ren told Rey her parents were

00:05:33.190 --> 00:05:38.999
just junk traders who sold her off to a slaver
on Jakku, but many fans believe Kylo was lying.

00:05:38.999 --> 00:05:44.159
So now we're facing a film literally called
The Rise of Skywalker, and Rey's action-heavy

00:05:44.159 --> 00:05:47.539
moments in the trailer certainly make her
look like a person on the rise.

00:05:47.540 --> 00:05:49.880
Is she the Skywalker the title refers to?

00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:54.560
Again, it feels too easy for a film this shrouded
in secrecy to give something like that away

00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:55.560
up front.

00:05:55.560 --> 00:05:56.720
And that brings us to...

00:05:57.680 --> 00:06:01.860
...the most popular theory to rise in the
wake of the new movie's title reveal.

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:05.941
According to the Internet hive-mind, now that
we've seen the last Jedi, it's time for the

00:06:05.941 --> 00:06:10.040
galaxy's Force users to call themselves something
new: Skywalkers.

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:15.060
The first two Star Wars trilogies ended with
films that featured the words "Jedi" and "Sith"

00:06:15.060 --> 00:06:18.840
in their respective titles, each concluding
with the return or the revenge of a group

00:06:18.840 --> 00:06:19.930
of Force users.

00:06:19.930 --> 00:06:24.610
The Last Jedi featured numerous references
to Luke Skywalker's belief that the Jedi were

00:06:24.610 --> 00:06:27.600
too self-righteous to be preserved and that
the order should end.

00:06:27.940 --> 00:06:32.000
Yoda confirmed that belief by destroying the
great tree at the heart of the first Jedi

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:34.280
temple, so the Jedi are indeed gone in some
form.

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:39.660
So what if Rey chooses a new direction, and
uses the name of her mentor as a label?

00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:43.330
What if "Skywalker" is not a person, but a
group of people?

00:06:43.330 --> 00:06:45.760
Now that's a rise that might be pretty interesting
indeed...

